  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalled frontal boundary will produce showers and a few\nthunderstorms over southern Virginia and northeast North Carolina\ntoday. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible.\n\nAs of 0230Z, a frontal boundary is stalled across far southern \nVA early this morning with some isolated showers ongoing over \nthe Peninsulas and also VA Beach south into NE NC. This boundary\nwill remain fairly stationary through the day today, before \nlifting back north as a warm from by this evening and \ntonight/early Tues AM. This feature will be the focus for shower\nand isolated strong tstm development again today, primarily \nthis afternoon/evening, There is a low end SVR threat today, \nmainly for a few instances of damaging winds, and also a SLGT \nERO for all of NE NC and a marginal ERO across southern VA for \nisolated flash flooding due to heavy rainfall potential. Dew \npoints will be highest across southern areas today (mid-70s) \nwith PWATs in excess of 2\", hence the MRGL/SLGT ERO. Highs today\nwill be in the mid 80s with the some low 80s along the \nimmediate coast. As the warm front lifts north tonight, we'll \nsee scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms and locally \nheavy rainfall will be possible. Lows tonight in the upr 60s to \nmid 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periods of unsettled weather are expected Tuesday\nthrough much of the coming week. Temperatures will trend below \nnormal by Thursday and Friday.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, the severe threat looks to be \nincreasing as a much stronger trough will start to move out of \nthe Midwest and into the Mid-Atlantic. Tuesday looks to be the \nbest SVR day as shear looks to be the strongest and multiple \nshortwaves will move through which could initiate storms. Some \nof the wind profiles based of model soundings show the potential\nfor discrete storms ahead of a possible line of storms coming \nout of the NW. At the surface, a strong cold front will move \ninto place Wednesday helping to initiate thunderstorms \npotentially producing another round of severe weather. There \nremains slight uncertainty but models have hinted on the \npotential for a recovering atmosphere ahead of the front that is\nfavorable for severe weather. The primary hazard at this time \nlooks to be strong to severe wind gusts but other hazards could \npotentially evolve. For both Tuesday and Wednesday, most of the\narea will be under a slight ERO. With PW's remaining high and\nshowers and thunderstorms continuing, the flood risk could\nincrease, especially for areas that receive multiple rounds of\nheavy rain. The QPF forecast at this time calls for 2-4\" of rain\nbasically from RIC on south and east through Wed night. Once \nthis cold front moves through, slightly cooler (below normal) \ntemperatures are favored for Thursday/Friday. However, the risk \nof heavy rain due to additional showers and thunderstorms looks \nto continue across southeast VA and northeast NC through the end\nof the week.",
